- **Reasoning:** The question asks how to insert a blurred image as a background. To achieve this, we need to use CSS for the blurring effect and HTML for the image insertion.
- **Solution:** 
  ```html
  <!doctype html>
  <html lang="fr">
  <head>
      <meta charset="UTF-8">
      <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
      <title>Document</title>
      <style>
          .blur-background {
              background-image: url('image.jpg');
              background-size: cover;
              background-position: center;
              filter: blur(5px);
              height: 100vh;
              width: 100vw;
          }
      </style>
  </head>
  <body>
      <div class="blur-background"></div>
  </body>
  </html>
  ```
- **Justification:** The `<img>` tag is used to insert the image, but since we want it as a background, we use a `<div>` element styled with CSS. The `filter: blur(5px);` property applies a blur effect to the image, making it appear as a background. The `background-image`, `background-size`, and `background-position` properties are used to set the image as the background of the `<div>`.

- **Reasoning:** The question asks how to correctly set an image as a background in CSS, given that it is not working.
- **Solution:** The issue in the provided CSS code is that the `background-image` property is incorrectly using the `src()` function. The correct syntax for `background-image` is to directly use the `url()` function to specify the image path.
- **Solution:**
  ```css
  .blur-background {
      background-image: url("./images/img1.jpg");
      background-size: cover;
      background-position: center;
      filter: blur(5px);
      height: 100vh;
      width: 100vw;
  }
  ```
- **Justification:** The `url()` function is used to specify the path to the image file, and it is the correct way to set an image as a background in CSS. Using `src()` is incorrect in this context and would not work.

- **Reasoning:** The question asks how to ensure that the background image is behind the foreground image.
- **Solution:** To ensure that the background image is behind the foreground image, you need to adjust the `z-index` property in the CSS. The `z-index` property controls the stack order of elements. Elements with a higher `z-index` value will be displayed in front of elements with a lower `z-index` value.
- **Solution:**
  ```css
  .blur-background {
      background-image: url("./images/img1.jpg");
      background-size: cover;
      background-position: center;
      filter: blur(5px);
      height: 100vh;
      width: 100vw;
      z-index: -1; /* Ensure this is behind other elements */
  }

  img {
      z-index: 1; /* Ensure this is in front of the background */
  }
  ```
- **Justification:** By setting the `z-index` of the `.blur-background` to `-1` and the `z-index` of the `<img>` to `1`, you ensure that the background image is behind the foreground image. This is a common method to control the stacking order of elements in CSS.
